**Read:** [Vacuum systems — Vincent Baglin and Roberto Kersevan](../sources/Vacuum-Systems-Baglin-Kersevan.lingua#r_1K9ATEHFM7ET2H8TRHC3QNCRFN), II.8.3.3; [Fundamentals of Vacuum Technology — Walter Umrath and contributors](../sources/Fundamentals-of-Vacuum-Technology-Leybold.lingua#r_1S93A5X32978GSHZH4CF96VPQR), sputter-ion pumps in section 2.1.8.

An ion pump combines electron motion, gas ionization and interactions with electrodes. Reactive sputtered material can bind some gases; implantation and burial contribute for other species. The pump stores material internally and has no ordinary continuous exhaust path.

Capture and release depend on gas species, electrode state, discharge behavior and operating pressure. Pump current can provide a pressure-related signal only with a suitable calibration and interpretation. A current change can also reflect a changed gas composition or discharge condition.

**Work:** Draw the paths of electrons, ions and neutral molecules and identify where energy and material enter the electrodes. Compare chemically active and inert constituents. Explain why a nominal pumping-speed rating and a current-to-pressure conversion need their own conditions, and how accumulated gas and electrode erosion affect continuing operation.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies — from molecules to useful service](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
